.TH FSLEDGER 1 "2026-10-22" "fsledger" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fsledger \- maintain Git histories and filesystem integrity inventories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsledger check
.RB [ \-\-effective ]
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger
.BR doctor | status
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger run
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.br
.B fsledger
.BR flush | verify
.BI \-\-repository " NAME"
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger report preview
.BI \-\-repository " NAME"
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger outbox clear
.BI \-\-repository " NAME"
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger restore
.BI \-\-repository " NAME"
.BI \-\-date " DATE"
.RB [ \-\-overwrite " off|always|newer" ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.I SOURCE
.RI [ DESTINATION ]
.RB [ \-c " configuration" ]
.br
.B fsledger \-\-help
.br
.B fsledger \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
fsledger mirrors configured source paths into private Git repositories.
Source paths are relative to / inside each repository. Source directories never
receive Git metadata. Changes without a known actor are still committed.
Git must be installed for Git repositories. DB repositories store hashes and
metadata without file contents. Root is optional when sources are readable and
state
locations are writable; fanotify and Audit require additional privileges.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Validate strict YAML, source patterns, exclusions and location conflicts.
Render notification templates as described in fsledger.yaml(5).
Report how many sources are present, absent or skipped; absent ones are valid.
Does not create files, log destinations or repositories. --effective prints
resolved repository settings with DSN and webhook credentials removed.
.TP
.B doctor
Print JSON describing the kernel, mounts, watcher capabilities and passive
Audit
transport access. Only sources that currently exist are probed; absent and
skipped entries are listed as missing_sources and skipped_sources. Opening an
Audit socket does not establish rule coverage.
A fanotify detector is reported only when its marks install on the whole tree,
as the daemon installs them, so probing a large tree takes as long as a start.
When a selected fanotify group enables PIDFD, fanotify_pidfd_lifetime tests
delivery after a writer is reaped. checked=false means the probe was
inconclusive;
checked=true with available=false confirms the missing capability.
The probe writes and removes one private temporary file beneath runtime using a
fixed /bin/sh script; it does not write source files. If runtime does not exist,
doctor reports an inconclusive result. The daemon shares the first conclusive
result across its repositories; inconclusive probes may be retried. Affected
repositories record warnings in their log and status.
A missing capability does not stop monitoring. Warnings link to the kernel fix:
.UR https://lore.kernel.org/linux-fsdevel/20260607003343.425939-1-anonymemeow@gmail.com/#t
fanotify PIDFD lifetime patch
.UE
Kernel versions alone cannot identify backported fixes. This test does not
validate cgroup resolution or guarantee actor identity.
For explicit polling, capabilities_probed=false: watcher groups and the PIDFD
lifetime probe are not opened.
.TP
.B status
Print one JSON object per repository from local status snapshots. paths are
the sources selected at the snapshot time; missing_sources, skipped_sources and
unavailable_sources explain the rest, and unmounted_paths lists the recorded
mount points whose filesystem is not mounted: the sources and trees they hold
are set aside.
scan describes the current daemon's scan times, duration, result, observed
entries,
hashes computed by the catalog scan and upcoming ordinary/full schedules.
catalog.last_full_scan persists the last complete scan across restarts.
A full scan whose result is partial because of unstable_paths does not update
it, and scan.initialized remains false until a full scan completes.
Snapshots are published atomically but are volatile, without a durability
guarantee across machine restarts. The daemon refreshes each snapshot's file
time every second while its repository runs. A file time older than five seconds
sets running=false and adds a stale warning; a fresh file with an updated
timestamp older than five seconds keeps running and adds a busy warning, as
during a long reconciliation.
A repository without a snapshot, because the daemon is not running or has not
started it yet, prints an object with only repository, type, running=false and
a no status snapshot warning; the remaining repositories are still printed.
catalog.pending_notifications counts queued notifications;
catalog.exhausted_notifications, omitted while zero, counts those that stopped
retrying and adds a warning until a restart retries them or outbox clear
discards them. See notifications in fsledger.yaml(5).
This command is not a live daemon RPC.
.TP
.B run
Run in the foreground until SIGINT or SIGTERM. Initialize repositories and, by
default, reconcile offline changes at startup. Explicit polling with
watch.reconcile.on_start=false defers observation to its scan schedule.
Git repositories commit an initial snapshot when representable content exists.
Never create empty commits.
.TP
.B flush --repository NAME
Queue an asynchronous request for the running Git worker to commit deferred
paths.
Also refresh the source selection and reconcile it. Check status.catalog for
deferred_paths and deferred_since; queuing does not confirm a commit or Git
publication. The request completes after one reconciliation; paths still listed
in unstable_paths are not waited for. Pending versions survive restart; the
volatile request may not.
.TP
.B verify --repository NAME
Rescan every source and recompute content hashes, comparing each entry with its
previous observation as described under integrity in fsledger.yaml(5).
Differences are recorded in the catalog and their notifications are queued;
the daemon sends them when it runs again. verify does not commit to the Git
mirror; the daemon's next reconciliation does. The repository daemon must be
stopped. Prints one JSON object: result (id, observed, hashed, changed) and
status, the catalog counters that status shows under catalog. It exits with
status 2 when it recorded changes, including every entry of a first
inventory. A Go runtime crash also exits with 2, so scripts also require the
JSON object. verify refuses to run while a source entry or pattern cannot be
read; see paths in fsledger.yaml(5).
.TP
.B report preview --repository NAME
Render the first pending scheduled-report part from an existing catalog opened
read-only. Stop the repository daemon first. No source scan, Git initialization,
AI call, mail delivery or report-cursor advancement occurs. See reports in
fsledger.yaml(5). Status includes report scheduling and delivery timestamps.
.TP
.B outbox clear --repository NAME
Discard every notification queued by the repository, including delayed
retries and those that stopped retrying, without sending it. Summary evidence
held for a discarded report is
released, but no delivery is recorded. Recorded changes are kept. The catalog
must already exist and the repository daemon must be stopped.
.TP
.B restore --repository NAME --date DATE [--overwrite off|always|newer] [--dry-run] SOURCE [DESTINATION]
Write the contents of SOURCE as recorded in the last commit at or before DATE
into DESTINATION on this host. SOURCE is a path inside the Git mirror, with or
without the leading slash; DESTINATION defaults to the original location. DATE
is YYYY-MM-DD (meaning the end of that day), YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM[:SS] in local
time, or RFC 3339. Existing entries are kept with
.B off
(the default), replaced with
.B always
keeping their mode and owner, or replaced only when the commit is newer with
.BR newer .
Type conflicts are reported and skipped, and the command then exits with an
error. Symbolic links are never followed: a destination directory that is or
lies below one is refused, and entries below a symbolic link or below a
directory that failed are reported as failed. Ownership, ACLs and extended
attributes are not restored. Only Git objects are read, so the daemon may keep
running.
.SH OPTIONS
Options follow the command and may appear before or after subcommands and
operands. Use -- to separate operands beginning with a dash. An option given to
a command that does not use it is an error.
.TP
.BI \-c " configuration"
Read this YAML file. Default: /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Override logging.level with debug for run. Does not change logging.file.
.TP
.B \-\-effective
With check, print the resolved settings and the current sources of every
repository instead of the summary line.
.TP
.BI \-\-repository " name"
Repository for flush, verify, report preview, outbox clear and restore.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Display help. Use after a command for its option help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, Git commit and Go toolchain on standard output and exit
without reading the configuration. Accepted alone, as the version command,
or after any command.
.SH LOGGING
The YAML keys logging.level and logging.file select severity and destination.
The default is info on standard error. A configured file is opened in append
mode; existing contents are retained. See
.BR fsledger.yaml (5).
JSON and check results use standard output. Configuration and startup failures
are reported on standard error; daemon failures also reach the configured log.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT , " SIGTERM"
Stop watchers, finish pending work and reconcile before exiting.
Explicit polling with watch.reconcile.on_stop=false omits the final traversal.
A reconciliation interrupted by shutdown is not repeated when startup
reconciliation will resume it. The 270-second grace is counted from the stop
request; blocked filesystem I/O may exceed it.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Log a notice at info level. Does not reload configuration or reopen logs.
Restart to apply changes or reopen a renamed log file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml
Default configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/fsledger/repos
Default private repository root.
.TP
.I /run/fsledger/REPOSITORY.json
Default local status snapshots.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success or clean shutdown; 1 on an invalid command, configuration error
or runtime failure; 2 when verify recorded changes. Doctor reports unavailable capabilities in JSON and may
succeed while individual capabilities are unavailable. Status likewise succeeds
with stale or missing snapshots; an unreadable or undecodable snapshot is a
failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fsledger check -c /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml
fsledger doctor -c /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml
fsledger run -c /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml --debug
fsledger status -c /etc/fsledger/fsledger.yaml
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsledgerd (8),
.BR fsledger.yaml (5),
.BR git (1)

.SH STORAGE
Catalogs use Pebble with Protobuf and Snappy. They retain current observations,
deferred commit decisions and the durable notification outbox. Optional reports
and inbound Git synchronization also retain pending journals and drafts until
their work is complete.
Git holds file history; the catalog keeps only the latest observation of each
path, with no permanent observation history.
